Interpret it as evidence of a narrow timing window, not as a failure of either study.

The five-minute interruption occurred while the underlying wash still had enough moisture to pull the dark pigment outward, producing a small bloom. The six-minute interruption arrived after the surface had dried enough to keep the mark contained, but that also made the mark slightly too crisp. Because both versions preserved the large cool wash and the warm doorway edge, interruption timing appears to be affecting the character of the dark rain mark more than the broader composition.

A controlled five-and-a-half-minute study is the most useful immediate next test. It directly interpolates between the two observed outcomes while keeping the comparison clean. Define the target beforehand as:

- wash remains intact;
- warm edge remains warm;
- dark mark stays legible;
- edges soften slightly;
- no visible bloom.

Do not treat the result as proof that 5.5 minutes is optimal. A half-minute difference may be meaningful, but watercolor timing can also be sensitive to room humidity, paper moisture, brush load, and the exact moment the mark contacts the surface. Record the 5.5-minute outcome as another observation.

If you have enough paper for a small sequence, the stronger test would be 5, 5.5, and 6 minutes, ideally with two studies at each timing. That would show whether the transition is gradual or whether the bloom/crisp boundary is abrupt. Keep the same paper orientation, paint preparation, brush load, wash size, and drying conditions, and judge all studies after they are fully dry.

So: yes, five-and-a-half minutes is the right next single test—but interpret it as locating the usable interval, not as confirming a final setting.

